  468  /*  DESCRIPTION OF THE DATABASE FORMAT                                 
  469                                                                         
  470                                                                         
  471     THE DATA FILE CONTAINS SEVERAL SECTIONS.  EACH BEGINS               
  472     WITH A LINE CONTAINING A NUMBER IDENTIFYING THE SECTION,            
  473     AND ENDS WITH A LINE CONTAINING "-1".                               
  474                                                                         
  475     SECTION 1:  LONG FORM DESCRIPTIONS.  EACH LINE CONTAINS A           
  476         LOCATION NUMBER, A TAB, AND A LINE OF TEXT.  THE SET            
  477         OF (NECESSARILY ADJACENT) LINES WHOSE NUMBERS ARE X             
  478         FORM THE LONG DESCRIPTION OF LOCATION X.                        
  479     SECTION 2:  SHORT FORM DESCRIPTIONS.  SAME FORMAT AS LONG           
  480         FORM.  NOT ALL PLACES HAVE SHORT DESCRIPTIONS.                  
  481     SECTION 3:  TRAVEL TABLE.  EACH LINE CONTAINS A LOCATION            
  482         NUMBER (X), A SECOND LOCATION NUMBER (Y), AND A LIST            
  483         OF MOTION NUMBERS (SEE SECTION 4).  EACH MOTION                 
  484         REPRESENTS A VERB WHICH WILL GO TO Y IF CURRENTLY AT            
  485         X.  Y, IN TURN, IS INTERPRETED AS FOLLOWS.  LET                 
  486         M=Y/1000, N=Y MOD 1000.                                         
  487                 IF N<=300       IT IS THE LOCATION TO GO TO.            
  488                 IF 300<N<=500   N-300 IS USED IN A COMPUTED             
  489                                 GOTO TO A SECTION OF SPECIAL            
  490                                 CODE.                                   
  491                 IF N>500        MESSAGE N-500 FROM SECTION 6            
  492                                 IS PRINTED, AND HE STAYS                
  493                                 WHEREVER HE IS.                         
  494         MEANWHILE, M SPECIFIES THE CONDITIONS ON THE MOTION.            
  495                 IF M=0          IT'S UNCONDITIONAL.                     
  496                 IF 0<M<100      IT IS DONE WITH M%                      
  497                                 PROBABILITY.                            
  498                 IF M=100        UNCONDITIONAL, BUT FORBIDDEN            
  499                                 TO DWARFS.                              
  500                 IF 100<M<=200   HE MUST BE CARRYING OBJECT              
  501                                 M-100.                                  
  502                 IF 200<M<=300   MUST BE CARRYING OR IN SAME             
  503                                 ROOM AS OBJECT M-200.                   
  504                 IF 300<M<=400   PROP(M MOD 100) MUST *NOT* BE           
  505                                 0.                                      
  506                 IF 400<M<=500   PROP(M MOD 100) MUST *NOT* BE           
  507                                 1.                                      
  508                 IF 500<M<=600   PROP(M MOD 100) MUST *NOT* BE           
  509                                 2, ETC.                                 
  510         IF THE CONDITION (IF ANY) IS NOT MET, THEN THE NEXT             
  511         *DIFFERENT* "DESTINATION" VALUE IS USED (UNLESS IT              
  512         FAILS TO MEET *ITS* CONDITIONS, IN WHICH CASE THE               
  513         NEXT IS FOUND, ETC.).  TYPICALLY, THE NEXT DEST WILL            
  514         BE FOR ONE OF THE SAME VERBS, SO THAT ITS ONLY USE IS           
  515         AS THE ALTERNATE DESTINATION FOR THOSE VERBS.  FOR              
  516         INSTANCE:                                                       
  517             15      110022  29      31      34      35      23      43  
  518             15      14      29                                          
  519         THIS SAYS THAT, FROM LOC 15, ANY OF THE VERBS 29, 31,           
  520         ETC., WILL TAKE HIM TO 22 IF HE'S CARRYING OBJECT 10,           
  521         AND OTHERWISE WILL GO TO 14.                                    
  522                 11      303008  49                                      
Aug 31, 2026  16:33     /***              ADVENTURE  -  PL/1 VERSION 4.0                  ***/                          
1.4.1                                               Source Listing                                           Page 10    
  523                 11      9       50                                      
  524         THIS SAYS THAT, FROM 11, 49 TAKES HIM TO 8 UNLESS               
  525         PROP(3)=0, IN WHICH CASE HE GOES TO 9.  VERB 50 TAKES           
  526         HIM TO 9 REGARDLESS OF PROP(3).                                 
  527     SECTION 4:  VOCABULARY.  EACH LINE CONTAINS A NUMBER (N),           
  528         A TAB, AND A FIVE-LETTER WORD.  CALL M=N/1000.  IF              
  529         M=0, THEN THE WORD IS A MOTION VERB FOR USE IN                  
  530         TRAVELLING (SEE SECTION 3).  ELSE, IF M=1, THE WORD             
  531         IS AN OBJECT.  ELSE, IF M=2, THE WORD IS AN ACTION              
  532         VERB (SUCH AS "CARRY" OR "ATTACK").  ELSE, IF M=3,              
  533         THE WORD IS A SPECIAL CASE VERB (SUCH AS "DIG") AND N           
  534         MOD 1000 IS AN INDEX INTO SECTION 6.  OBJECTS FROM 50           
  535         TO (CURRENTLY, ANYWAY) 79 ARE CONSIDERED TREASURES              
  536         (FOR PIRATE, CLOSEOUT).                                         
  537     SECTION 5:  OBJECT DESCRIPTIONS.  EACH LINE CONTAINS A              
  538         NUMBER (N), A TAB, AND A MESSAGE.  IF N IS FROM 1 TO            
  539         100, THE MESSAGE IS THE "INVENTORY" MESSAGE FOR                 
  540         OBJECT N.  OTHERWISE, N SHOULD BE 000, 100, 200,                
  541         ETC., AND THE MESSAGE SHOULD BE THE DESCRIPTION OF              
  542         THE PRECEDING OBJECT WHEN ITS PROP VALUE IS N/100.              
  543         THE N/100 IS USED ONLY TO DISTINGUISH MULTIPLE                  
  544         MESSAGES FROM MULTI-LINE MESSAGES- THE PROP INFO                
  545         ACTUALLY REQUIRES ALL MESSAGES FOR AN OBJECT TO BE              
  546         PRESENT AND CONSECUTIVE.  PROPERTIES WHICH PRODUCE NO           
  547         MESSAGE SHOULD BE GIVEN THE MESSAGE ">$<".                      
  548     SECTION 6:  ARBITRARY MESSAGES.  SAME FORMAT AS SECTIONS            
  549         1, 2, AND 5, EXCEPT THE NUMBERS BEAR NO RELATION TO             
  550         ANYTHING (EXCEPT FOR SPECIAL VERBS IN SECTION 4).               
  551     SECTION 7:  OBJECT LOCATIONS.  EACH LINE CONTAINS AN                
  552         OBJECT NUMBER AND ITS INITIAL LOCATION (ZERO (OR                
  553         OMITTED) IF NONE).  IF THE OBJECT IS IMMOVABLE, THE             
  554         LOCATION IS FOLLOWED BY A "-1".  IF IT HAS TWO                  
  555         LOCATIONS (E.G.  THE GRATE) THE FIRST LOCATION IS               
  556         FOLLOWED WITH THE SECOND, AND THE OBJECT IS ASSUMED             
  557         TO BE IMMOVABLE.                                                
  558     SECTION 8:  ACTION DEFAULTS.  EACH LINE CONTAINS AN                 
  559         "ACTION-VERB" NUMBER AND THE INDEX (IN SECTION 6) OF            
  560         THE DEFAULT MESSAGE FOR THE VERB.                               
  561     SECTION 9:  LIQUID ASSETS, ETC.  EACH LINE CONTAINS A               
  562         NUMBER (N) AND UP TO 20 LOCATION NUMBERS.  BIT N                
  563         (WHERE 0 IS THE UNITS BIT) IS SET IN COND(LOC) FOR              
  564         EACH LOC GIVEN.  THE COND BITS CURRENTLY ASSIGNED               
  565         ARE:                                                            
  566                 0       LIGHT                                           
  567                 1       IF BIT 2 IS ON:  ON FOR OIL, OFF FOR            
  568                         WATER                                           
  569                 2       LIQUID ASSET, SEE BIT 1                         
  570                 3       PIRATE DOESN'T GO HERE UNLESS                   
  571                         FOLLOWING PLAYER                                
  572         OTHER BITS ARE USED TO INDICATE AREAS OF INTEREST TO            
  573         "HINT" ROUTINES:                                                
  574                 4       TRYING TO GET INTO CAVE                         
  575                 5       TRYING TO CATCH BIRD                            
  576                 6       TRYING TO DEAL WITH SNAKE                       
  577                 7       LOST IN MAZE                                    
  578                 8       PONDERING DARK ROOM                             
  579                 9       AT WITT'S END                                   
  580         COND(LOC) IS SET TO 2, OVERRIDING ALL OTHER BITS, IF            
Aug 31, 2026  16:33     /***              ADVENTURE  -  PL/1 VERSION 4.0                  ***/                          
1.4.1                                               Source Listing                                           Page 11    
  581         LOC HAS FORCED MOTION.                                          
  582     SECTION 10:  CLASS MESSAGES.  EACH LINE CONTAINS A NUMBER           
  583         (N), A TAB, AND A MESSAGE DESCRIBING A CLASSIFICATION           
  584         OF PLAYER.  THE SCORING SECTION SELECTS THE                     
  585         APPROPRIATE MESSAGE, WHERE EACH MESSAGE IS CONSIDERED           
  586         TO APPLY TO PLAYERS WHOSE SCORES ARE HIGHER THAN THE            
  587         PREVIOUS N BUT NOT HIGHER THAN THIS N.  NOTE THAT               
  588         THESE SCORES PROBABLY CHANGE WITH EVERY MODIFICATION            
  589         (AND PARTICULARLY EXPANSION) OF THE PROGRAM.                    
  590     SECTION 11:  HINTS.  EACH LINE CONTAINS A HINT NUMBER               
  591         (CORRESPONDING TO A COND BIT, SEE SECTION 9), THE               
  592         NUMBER OF TURNS HE MUST BE AT THE RIGHT LOC(S) BEFORE           
  593         TRIGGERING THE HINT, THE POINTS DEDUCTED FOR TAKING             
  594         THE HINT, THE MESSAGE NUMBER (SECTION 6) OF THE                 
  595         QUESTION, AND THE MESSAGE NUMBER OF THE HINT.  THESE            
  596         VALUES ARE STASHED IN THE "HINTS" ARRAY.  HNTMAX IS             
  597         SET TO THE MAX HINT NUMBER (<= HNTSIZ).  NUMBERS 1-3            
  598         ARE UNUSABLE SINCE COND BITS ARE OTHERWISE ASSIGNED,            
  599         SO 2 IS USED TO REMEMBER IF HE'S READ THE CLUE IN THE           
  600         REPOSITORY, AND 3 IS USED TO REMEMBER WHETHER HE                
  601         ASKED FOR INSTRUCTIONS (GETS MORE TURNS, BUT LOSES              
  602         POINTS).                                                        
  603     SECTION 12:  MAGIC MESSAGES.  IDENTICAL TO SECTION 6                
  604         EXCEPT PUT IN A SEPARATE SECTION FOR EASIER                     
  605         REFERENCE.  MAGIC MESSAGES ARE USED BY THE STARTUP,             
  606         MAINTENANCE MODE, AND RELATED ROUTINES.                         
  607     SECTION 0:  END OF DATABASE.                                        
  608                                                                         
  609   */                                                                    
  610                                                                         
  611  ON ATTENTION BEGIN;                                                    
  612               ON ATTENTION SIGNAL ERROR;                                
  613               LMESSAGE = ' ';                                           
  614               ECTSWS(1) = '0'B;                       /* NO OPERANDS */ 
  615               ECTSWS(4) = '1'B;         /* LOGON OR LOGOFF REQUESTED */ 
  616               SUBSTR(LMESSAGE,1,1) = 'L';                               
  617               SUBSTR(LMESSAGE,2,1) = 'O';                               
  618               SUBSTR(LMESSAGE,3,1) = 'G';                               
  619               SUBSTR(LMESSAGE,4,1) = 'O';                               
  620               SUBSTR(LMESSAGE,5,1) = 'F';                               
  621               SUBSTR(LMESSAGE,6,1) = 'F';                               
  622               GO TO DEALLOC;                                            
  623               END;                                                      
  624                                                                         
  625  ON ERROR BEGIN;                                                        
  626           ON ERROR SYSTEM;                                              
  627           CALL BUG(99);                                                 
  628           END;                                                          
  629                                                                         
